<section class="hero"><div class="container"><div class="tag">JPSC PRELIMS • CUT OFF • SAFE SCORE</div><h1>Don't Chase One Number.<span> Build a Margin.</span></h1><p class="hero-text">Understand the difference between qualifying marks and the actual selection cut-off, study previous trends, interpret expected cut-offs carefully and use mock scores to build a realistic target.</p><div class="meta"><span>Updated: 17 August 2026</span><span>•</span><span>Habitat IAS</span><span>•</span><span>JPSC Civil Services</span></div><div class="buttons"><a class="btn yellow" href="#official">See Official Rule ↓</a><a class="btn outline" href="/mock-tests/free-gs/">Test Your Score →</a></div></div></section>
<section class="notice"><div class="container"><div class="notice-box"><strong>Official vs expected:</strong> JPSC's official examination documents should be treated as the authority for qualifying standards and declared cut-offs. Coaching estimates are useful only for planning. Never present an expected number as an official cut-off.</div></div></section>
<section class="stats"><div class="container grid four"><div><b>01</b><strong>Qualifying</strong><p>Minimum standard you must satisfy.</p></div><div><b>02</b><strong>Selection Cut-off</strong><p>Competitive score for the relevant selection list.</p></div><div><b>03</b><strong>Expected</strong><p>Unofficial estimate based on available evidence.</p></div><div><b>04</b><strong>Safe Target</strong><p>Your personal score target with a margin.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="content"><div class="container layout"><aside class="toc"><strong>ON THIS PAGE</strong><a href="#meaning">Cut-off vs qualifying</a><a href="#official">Official JPSC rule</a><a href="#previous">Previous cut-off</a><a href="#expected">Expected cut-off</a><a href="#safe">Safe score</a><a href="#category">Category strategy</a><a href="#score">Calculate score</a><a href="#mock">Mock strategy</a><a href="#pyq">PYQ strategy</a><a href="#mistakes">Common mistakes</a><a href="#faq">FAQs</a></aside><article>
<p class="lead">The most important cut-off lesson is simple: <strong>the qualifying mark and the competitive selection cut-off are not the same thing.</strong></p>
<h2 id="meaning">1. JPSC Cut-off vs Minimum Qualifying Marks</h2><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><small>MINIMUM QUALIFYING STANDARD</small><h3>Eligibility threshold</h3><p>A candidate must meet the prescribed minimum standard to remain eligible under the applicable rules.</p></div><div class="card"><small>CATEGORY CUT-OFF</small><h3>Competitive threshold</h3><p>The actual selection threshold can be higher because candidates are ranked for progression to the Main Written Examination.</p></div></div>
<div class="formula"><strong>Qualifying marks ≠ final competitive cut-off.</strong><span>Never use one as a substitute for the other.</span></div>
<h2 id="official">2. What the Official JPSC Examination Rule Says</h2><p>The official JPSC Combined Civil Services advertisement for the 2024 cycle states that the Preliminary Examination has two compulsory papers of 200 marks each. It also states that the Main selection list is prepared on the basis of total marks in both papers, subject to the prescribed minimum qualifying standard.</p><div class="official"><div><small>OFFICIAL DOCUMENT</small><strong>JPSC Combined Civil Services Advertisement</strong><p>Read the examination plan and selection-list methodology in the Commission's own document.</p></div><a class="btn blue" href="https://www.jpsc.gov.in/data/Advertisement_01_24_dated_27_01_2024.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Official PDF →</a></div><div class="grid four"><div class="card"><b>2 PAPERS</b><p>General Studies I + General Studies II.</p></div><div class="card"><b>200 + 200</b><p>200 marks for each paper in the cited scheme.</p></div><div class="card"><b>400 TOTAL</b><p>Total Preliminary marks in that scheme.</p></div><div class="card"><b>15× LIST</b><p>Initial selection list approximately 15 times advertised vacancies.</p></div></div><p>The advertisement says the marks obtained by the last candidate in the initial list are considered the UNR cut-off, and category cut-offs can be reduced where necessary to ensure adequate representation.</p>
<h2 id="previous">3. Previous-Year Cut-off: How to Read It</h2><p>Previous-year cut-offs are useful historical benchmarks, but compare them carefully. Check the stage, examination cycle, total marks, category and whether the number is official.</p><div class="table"><div class="head"><span>CHECK</span><span>WHY IT MATTERS</span></div><div><b>Stage</b><span>Prelims, Mains and final cut-offs are different.</span></div><div><b>Advertisement</b><span>Different cycles can have different vacancy and rule contexts.</span></div><div><b>Total marks</b><span>A raw number is meaningless without the denominator.</span></div><div><b>Category</b><span>UR, ST, SC, BC/EBC, EWS and horizontal categories can differ.</span></div><div><b>Official status</b><span>Verify whether the number is declared by JPSC or estimated elsewhere.</span></div></div><p>JPSC publishes separate cut-off documents for different stages and cycles. For example, its official 2025 Combined Civil Services document explicitly identifies its figures as Main Written Examination cut-offs; those figures must not be presented as Preliminary cut-offs.</p>
<h2 id="expected">4. What Is an Expected JPSC Cut-off?</h2><p>An expected cut-off is an estimate made before the official cut-off is available. It may consider paper difficulty, candidate reactions, vacancies, historical ranges and exam analysis.</p><div class="grid three"><div class="card"><b>USEFUL FOR</b><p>Setting a provisional preparation target.</p></div><div class="card"><b>NOT FOR</b><p>Claiming a number guarantees selection.</p></div><div class="card"><b>UPDATE</b><p>Revise the target when official information becomes available.</p></div></div><div class="warning"><strong>Never write “JPSC 2026 cut-off is X” unless JPSC has officially declared X for that examination stage.</strong> If discussing an estimate, label it clearly as expected or unofficial.</div>
<h2 id="safe">5. What Is a Safe Score?</h2><p>A safe score is not an official JPSC category. It is a preparation concept: a target above the uncertain competitive boundary.</p><div class="grid three"><div class="card"><b>LOW TARGET</b><p>Just around an estimated cut-off.</p><strong>High uncertainty</strong></div><div class="card"><b>WORKING TARGET</b><p>Above the estimated range with some margin.</p><strong>Better</strong></div><div class="card"><b>STRONG TARGET</b><p>Consistently high across multiple mocks.</p><strong>Best position</strong></div></div><div class="formula"><strong>Do not ask “What is the cut-off?” only.</strong><span>Ask “What score can I reproduce consistently under exam conditions?”</span></div>
<h2 id="category">6. Category-wise Cut-off: Check Your Own Category</h2><p>A candidate should not use an UR cut-off as a universal benchmark. Official JPSC documents can show separate vertical and horizontal category thresholds. Always check the relevant notification and result document.</p><div class="grid six"><div class="card"><b>UR</b><p>Unreserved.</p></div><div class="card"><b>ST</b><p>Scheduled Tribe.</p></div><div class="card"><b>SC</b><p>Scheduled Caste.</p></div><div class="card"><b>BC / EBC</b><p>Applicable backward categories.</p></div><div class="card"><b>EWS</b><p>Where applicable.</p></div><div class="card"><b>HORIZONTAL</b><p>Additional dimensions where prescribed.</p></div></div>
<h2 id="score">7. How to Calculate Your JPSC Prelims Mock Score</h2><p>In the cited 2024 scheme, each Preliminary paper carries 200 marks. Use the marking instructions applicable to the examination you are actually preparing for before calculating your score.</p><div class="grid four"><div class="card"><b>CORRECT</b><p>Apply marks per correct answer.</p></div><div class="card"><b>WRONG</b><p>Apply the current exam's penalty rule, if any.</p></div><div class="card"><b>UNANSWERED</b><p>Apply the current scoring rule.</p></div><div class="card"><b>TOTAL</b><p>Compare the resulting score with your target.</p></div></div><div class="warning"><strong>Do not copy a scoring formula from an old cycle.</strong> Always confirm the current notification's marking scheme.</div>
<h2 id="mock">8. Use Mock Tests to Build a Score Margin</h2><div class="grid four"><div class="card"><b>BEFORE</b><p>Set a target score and time strategy.</p></div><div class="card"><b>DURING</b><p>Attempt under realistic conditions.</p></div><div class="card"><b>AFTER</b><p>Analyse wrong, guessed and slow questions.</p></div><div class="card"><b>REPEAT</b><p>Repair weaknesses and test again.</p></div></div><div class="conversion"><div><small>HABITAT IAS • JPSC MOCK TESTS</small><h2>Don't wait for the cut-off to discover your score.</h2><p>Build a score history before the real examination.</p></div><div class="cta"><a class="btn yellow" href="/mock-tests/free-gs/">Attempt Free Mock →</a><a class="btn outline" href="/mock-tests/">View Mock Tests →</a></div></div>
<h2 id="pyq">9. Why PYQs Matter More Than Cut-off Rumours</h2><div class="grid two"><div class="card"><small>PYQ</small><h3>Preparation evidence</h3><ul><li>Actual historical questions</li><li>Question style</li><li>Topic recurrence</li><li>Difficulty context</li></ul></div><div class="card"><small>CUT-OFF</small><h3>Competition evidence</h3><ul><li>Historical selection boundary</li><li>Category context</li><li>Performance benchmark</li><li>Target-setting input</li></ul></div></div><div class="conversion"><div><small>HABITAT IAS • JPSC PRELIMS DECODED</small><h2>Decode the questions before chasing the score.</h2><p>Topic-wise PYQ study helps you understand the exam's historical demand.</p></div><div class="cta"><a class="btn yellow" href="/book/">Explore PYQ Book →</a><a class="btn outline" href="/book/#sample">View Sample →</a></div></div>
<h2>10. Why Cut-off Changes</h2><div class="grid three"><div class="card"><b>VACANCIES</b><p>Selection capacity can influence competition.</p></div><div class="card"><b>PAPER DIFFICULTY</b><p>Harder papers can shift score distributions.</p></div><div class="card"><b>CANDIDATE PERFORMANCE</b><p>Overall score distribution matters.</p></div><div class="card"><b>EXAM SCHEME</b><p>Rules and marking must be checked for the cycle.</p></div><div class="card"><b>CATEGORY REPRESENTATION</b><p>Selection methodology can affect category thresholds.</p></div><div class="card"><b>VACANCY NOTIFICATION</b><p>Different cycles have different contexts.</p></div></div>
<h2>11. Five Questions Before Trusting Any Cut-off Table</h2><div class="grid five"><div class="card"><b>1</b><p>Which cycle?</p></div><div class="card"><b>2</b><p>Which stage?</p></div><div class="card"><b>3</b><p>What total marks?</p></div><div class="card"><b>4</b><p>Which category?</p></div><div class="card"><b>5</b><p>Official or estimated?</p></div></div>
<h2>12. Never Compare Prelims With Mains</h2><p>Search results often place different stages together. JPSC's official website publishes separate documents. Its official 2025 Combined Civil Services document identifies its figures as Main Written Examination cut-offs, so those figures cannot be presented as Prelims cut-offs.</p><div class="grid three"><div class="card"><b>PRELIMS</b><p>Objective stage under the applicable scheme.</p></div><div class="card"><b>MAINS</b><p>Written examination with a different marking structure.</p></div><div class="card"><b>FINAL</b><p>Final service/category outcome can use another merit calculation.</p></div></div>
<h2>13. Practical Safe-score Method</h2><div class="grid three"><div class="card"><b>01</b><p>Collect official historical cut-offs for comparable cycles.</p></div><div class="card"><b>02</b><p>Separate categories and examination stages.</p></div><div class="card"><b>03</b><p>Observe the range instead of one isolated number.</p></div><div class="card"><b>04</b><p>Set a provisional target above the estimated boundary.</p></div><div class="card"><b>05</b><p>Test that target across multiple mocks.</p></div><div class="card"><b>06</b><p>Increase the margin if scores fluctuate heavily.</p></div></div>
<h2>14. If Your Mock Score Is Below Target</h2><div class="grid three"><div class="card"><b>LOW ACCURACY</b><p>Fix concepts and question interpretation.</p></div><div class="card"><b>HIGH GUESSING</b><p>Improve elimination discipline.</p></div><div class="card"><b>SLOW SPEED</b><p>Practise timed tests.</p></div><div class="card"><b>SUBJECT GAP</b><p>Reallocate study time.</p></div><div class="card"><b>CARELESS ERRORS</b><p>Improve question-reading habits.</p></div><div class="card"><b>INCONSISTENCY</b><p>Analyse more mocks.</p></div></div>
<h2>15. Last-phase Cut-off Strategy</h2><div class="grid three"><div class="phase"><small>EARLY</small><strong>Coverage</strong><p>Syllabus + PYQs.</p></div><div class="phase"><small>MIDDLE</small><strong>Accuracy</strong><p>Topic tests + mocks.</p></div><div class="phase"><small>FINAL</small><strong>Consistency</strong><p>Full mocks + error log.</p></div></div>
<h2 id="mistakes">16. Common Cut-off Mistakes</h2><div class="mistakes"><div><b>01</b><span>Preparing around an unofficial number.</span></div><div><b>02</b><span>Confusing qualifying marks with selection cut-off.</span></div><div><b>03</b><span>Mixing Prelims, Mains and final cut-offs.</span></div><div><b>04</b><span>Ignoring the total marks.</span></div><div><b>05</b><span>Using another category's benchmark.</span></div><div><b>06</b><span>Judging readiness from one mock.</span></div><div><b>07</b><span>Ignoring PYQs while chasing estimates.</span></div><div><b>08</b><span>Not maintaining a score trend.</span></div></div>
<h2>17. Final JPSC Cut-off Checklist</h2><div class="checklist"><div><b>01</b><span>Current notification checked.</span></div><div><b>02</b><span>Qualifying vs selection separated.</span></div><div><b>03</b><span>Correct examination stage checked.</span></div><div><b>04</b><span>Total marks checked.</span></div><div><b>05</b><span>Own category checked.</span></div><div><b>06</b><span>Official historical cut-offs used.</span></div><div><b>07</b><span>Unofficial estimates labelled.</span></div><div><b>08</b><span>Target includes a margin.</span></div><div><b>09</b><span>Several mocks tracked.</span></div><div><b>10</b><span>Every mock analysed.</span></div><div><b>11</b><span>PYQs guide preparation.</span></div><div><b>12</b><span>Study plan updated from evidence.</span></div></div>
<h2>18. Final Answer: What Score Should You Target?</h2><p>There is no responsible single number that can guarantee JPSC Preliminary selection in advance. The correct target depends on the examination cycle, paper difficulty, competition, vacancies, category and applicable rules.</p><div class="quote"><strong>Don't prepare to barely cross the cut-off.</strong><span>Prepare to create a repeatable score margin.</span></div>
<div class="conversion"><div><small>HABITAT IAS • JPSC PREPARATION</small><h2>Know your score before the result tells you.</h2><p>Solve PYQs, take mocks, analyse errors and build your margin.</p></div><div class="cta"><a class="btn yellow" href="/mock-tests/free-gs/">Attempt Free Mock →</a><a class="btn outline" href="/book/">JPSC PYQ Book →</a></div></div>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="faq">{faqs.map(item=><details><summary>{item.q}</summary><p>{item.a}</p></details>)}</div>
</article></div></section>
